    </ol>
	</objectives>

  <subsection>
    <title>Why bother with the <m>xy</m>-plane?</title>

    <p>
    It is rare to have an application where we only deal with one quantity (one number) at a time.
    Often, the value of one quantity has a relationship to the value of another quantity.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            For example, suppose you are driving on a road where travel at <m>60</m> miles per hour.
            If <m>x</m> represents the number of hours you have been driving and <m>y</m> represents the number of miles you have traveled, 
            then we have the relationship <m>y = 60x</m>. 
            Having an equation like this involving two variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> allows us to describe the specific connection between these two quantities.
            In this example, we will later see that the point <m>(x,y) = (31,1860)</m> is relevant for the equation <m>y=60x</m>, and this means that 
            having this point be relevant means that if you drove a total of <m>31</m> hours on a road trip, then you would have traveled a total of <m>1860</m> miles.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            As another example, suppose you need to track the height of a plant as it grows.
            If <m>x</m> represents the number of weeks since you planted the seed and <m>y</m> represents the height of the plant in inches, 
            the relationship between these two quantities might be described by the equation <m>y = 0.04x^2</m> mentioning both variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
            In this example, the point <m>(x,y) = (10,4)</m> is relevant for the equation <m>y = 0.04x^2</m>, 
            which means that <m>10</m> weeks after planting the seed, the height of the plant is <m>4</m> inches.
            The point <m>(x,y) = (30,36)</m> is also relevant, which means that <m>30</m> weeks after planting the seed, the height of the plant is <m>36</m> inches.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      The last example hints at the power of having an equation with two variables.
      We can plug in a number for <m>x</m> and get a number for <m>y</m>.
      But then we can plug in a <emph>different</emph> number for <m>x</m> and get the relevant <m>y</m>-value for this new <m>x</m>-value.
      The idea that each <m>x</m>-value leads to a different <m>y</m>-value means that this relationship can be described algebraically with an equation, but also geometrically with a graph on the <m>xy</m>-plane.
    </p>

  </subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title>The <m>xy</m>-plane</title>

    <p>
      The <m>xy</m>-plane (also called the Cartesian plane) has two lines (which are called axes) that are perpendicular to each other.
      The horizontal line is called the <em><m>x</m>-axis</em> and the vertical line is called the <em><m>y</m>-axis</em>.
      Each axis is labeled with the letter of the variable it represents: the <m>x</m>-axis is labeled on the right and the <m>y</m>-axis is labeled at the top.
      The two axes meet at a point called the <em>origin</em>, which can be labeled with the letter <m>O</m>. Here is a minimal picture of the <m>xy</m>-plane:
      
      <figure>
        <caption>Minimal picture of the <m>xy</m>-plane</caption>
        <image width="35%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[white] (-2,2) rectangle (2,2);
                \draw[<->] (-2,0) -- (2,0);
                \node at (2,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-2) -- (0,2);
                \node at (.4,2) {$y$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      The axes divide the plane into four regions called <em>quadrants</em>.
      Quadrant I is the upper right region, Quadrant II is the upper left region, Quadrant III is the lower left region, and Quadrant IV is the lower right region.
      The axes themselves are not part of any quadrant.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once the axes are drawn, we can draw points in the plane.
      A point is communicated by writing what is called an <em>ordered pair</em>.
      An ordered pair is written in the form <m>(x,y)</m>, where <m>x</m> is the <em><m>x</m>-coordinate</em> of the point and <m>y</m> is the <em><m>y</m>-coordinate</em> of the point.
      For clarity, we will always write the <m>x</m>-coordinate first and the <m>y</m>-coordinate second (so note that these are in alphabetical order: x followed by y next), with a comma between the numbers to separate, and surrounded in parentheses.
      The <m>x</m>-coordinate is a number, and the <m>y</m>-coordinate is a number.
      So, when a point is written, we always write a left parenthesis, then a number for the <m>x</m>-coordinate, then a comma, then a number for the <m>y</m>-coordinate, and then a right parenthesis.
      For example, writing <m>(7,2)</m> is an example of a point, and for this point, the <m>x</m>-coordinate is <m>7</m> and the <m>y</m>-coordinate is <m>2</m>.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <m>x</m>-coordinate is the horizontal coordinate, since it tells us how far to the left and right the point is from the origin.
            If the <m>x</m>-coordinate is positive, then the point is to the right of the origin;
            if the <m>x</m>-coordinate is negative, then the point is to the left of the origin;
            and if the <m>x</m>-coordinate is zero, then we do not need to move to the left or right from the origin, so the point would be on the <m>y</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <m>y</m>-coordinate is the vertical coordinate, since it tells us how far up and down the point is from the origin.
            If the <m>y</m>-coordinate is positive, then the point is above the origin;
            if the <m>y</m>-coordinate is negative, then the point is below the origin;
            and if the <m>y</m>-coordinate is zero, then we do not need to move up or down from the origin, so the point would be on the <m>x</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      
    </p>

    <principle>
      <title>text</title>
      <p>
        text
      </p>
    </principle>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the equation <m>3x+1 = 9</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          First, we subtract <m>1</m> from both sides to get <m>3x = 8</m>.
          Next, we divide both sides by <m>3</m> to get <m>x = \frac{8}{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

